          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My dad is 80, confused since this morning and has not passed water since yesterday. How urgent is this, and what would be looked for?",
          "answer": "**Please get urgent medical help now.** Call your local emergency number, or take your dad to the nearest emergency department. Don't wait to see if it passes. New confusion in an 80-year-old, combined with no urine for about a day, needs same-day assessment.\n\nThe risk check returned a \"routine\" label, but I don't think you should rely on that. It matched none of its listed emergency patterns, and it says that is not a clearance. The guidance I found points the other way:\n\n- **New confusion needs urgent assessment.** The urinary and respiratory guidance lists confusion among the symptoms that require urgent assessment (*Upper respiratory infection*, General clinical reference).\n- **Older adults can be seriously unwell with few obvious signs.** The guidance on acute abdominal pain notes that older adults can have serious illness with unimpressive examination findings and little fever. It says the threshold for assessment in these groups is lower (*Acute abdominal pain that needs urgent assessment*, Surgical and emergency medicine society guidance).\n- **Sepsis can be missed at first.** Its early signs can look like an ordinary infection. The guidance says family members should directly ask, \"could this be sepsis?\", because that prompts a structured assessment. It also says a family's sense that someone is unusually unwell is an important early signal (*Sepsis: signs of a life-threatening response to infection*, Critical care and emergency medicine society guidance).\n\nTell the emergency team exactly what you told me: his age, the confusion starting this morning, and that he has not passed urine since yesterday. Ask them directly whether it could be sepsis.\n\n**What the retrieved material does not cover:** none of the passages describe what clinicians would check for this combination of confusion and reduced urine output, or which tests they would use. The urinary infection guidance says a urine test alone does not explain confusion in an older person. A clinician needs to assess him in person.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
